Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. COUNTING HOUSE - DAY

SCROOGE sits at his desk, cold and sharp as flint. BOB CRATCHIT writes in a dismal tank, his fire a single coal.

FRED bursts in, glowing from the fog.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Don't be cross, uncle!

SCROOGE
What else can I be, when I live in such a world of fools as this? If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with Merry Christmas on his lips should be boiled with his own pudding, and buried with a stake of holly through his heart.

FRED
Uncle!

SCROOGE
Nephew! Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

FRED
But you don't keep it.

SCROOGE
Let me leave it alone, then. Good afternoon!

FRED
I want nothing from you; I ask nothing of you; why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
A Merry Christmas, uncle!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves. Two PORTLY GENTLEMEN enter with books and papers.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge, or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago, this very night.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
At this festive season of the year, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute.

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
They are. Still.

SCROOGE
The Treadmill and the Poor Law are in full vigour, then?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Both very busy, sir.

SCROOGE
I'm very glad to hear it.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
A few of us are endeavouring to raise a fund to buy the Poor some meat and drink, and means of warmth. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing! I wish to be left alone. I don't make merry myself at Christmas and I can't afford to make idle people merry. Those who are badly off must go there.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Many can't go there; and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

The gentlemen withdraw. Scrooge resumes his labours.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Bob promises and leaves. Scrooge walks out with a growl.

EXT. LONDON STREET - NIGHT

Scrooge walks through fog and frost. A caroller begins at his keyhole.

CAROLLER
God bless you, merry gentleman! May nothing you dismay!

Scrooge seizes a ruler. The singer flees.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Scrooge unlocks his door. The knocker becomes MARLEY'S FACE, then a knocker again. Scrooge enters, lights a candle, checks his rooms.

He sits before a low fire with gruel. A bell begins to swing. Every bell in the house rings. A clanking noise rises from below. The cellar door flies open. Chains drag up the stairs.

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

MARLEY'S GHOST passes through the door, transparent, bound in cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers. The flame leaps up.

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

MARLEY'S GHOST
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
Can you sit down?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I can.

The Ghost sits.

MARLEY'S GHOST
You don't believe in me.

SCROOGE
I don't. A slight disorder of the stomach makes them cheats. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

The Ghost raises a frightful cry and shakes its chain. It removes its bandage; its jaw drops upon its breast. Scrooge falls to his knees.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Man of the worldly mind! Do you believe in me or not?

SCROOGE
I do. I must. But why do spirits walk the earth, and why do they come to me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen, and travel far and wide; and if that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death.

SCROOGE
You are fettered. Tell me why?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard. Or would you know the weight and length of the strong coil you bear yourself? It was full as heavy and as long as this, seven Christmas Eves ago. You have laboured on it, since.

SCROOGE
Jacob, Old Jacob Marley, tell me more. Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY'S GHOST
I have none to give. My spirit never walked beyond our counting-house. Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were, all, my business.

SCROOGE
But you were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Business! Mankind was my business.

MARLEY'S GHOST
At this time of the rolling year, I suffer most. I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
Is that the chance and hope you mentioned, Jacob?

MARLEY'S GHOST
It is.

SCROOGE
I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread. Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One. Expect the second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate. Look to see me no more.

The Ghost walks backward to the window, which opens. It beckons, then floats out. Scrooge sees phantoms wandering, all in chains. He closes the window and goes to bed.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge wakes. The clock strikes one. Curtains draw aside. The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST stands before him, like a child yet an old man, white hair, tunic of purest white, jet of light from its head.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

SCROOGE
Lead me where you will!

They pass through the wall to an open country road.

EXT. SCHOOL ROAD - DAY

SCROOGE
I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

They reach a mansion. Inside, a lonely boy reads by a feeble fire. Scrooge weeps.

SCROOGE
Poor boy!

A little girl, FAN, runs in.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all. Father is so much kinder than he used to be. You're to be a man, and are never to come back here.

They leave in a chaise.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had one child. Your nephew.

They enter a warehouse. OLD FEZZIWIG calls out.

OLD FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick!

Young Scrooge and DICK WILKINS enter. Fezziwig shuts the shutters. The room becomes a ballroom. Fiddler plays. Mrs. Fezziwig and the Miss Fezziwigs dance. Everyone joins.

SCROOGE
Why! Is it not? He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome; a pleasure or a toil. The happiness he gives, is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

The Ghost shows Belle. She releases young Scrooge.

BELLE
May you be happy in the life you have chosen!

Scrooge sees Belle, now a matron, with her daughter and many children. Her husband mentions seeing Scrooge alone.

SCROOGE
Remove me! I cannot bear it!

He seizes the extinguisher-cap and presses it down. The light streams from under it. Scrooge sinks into sleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge wakes. The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT sits on a throne of food, torch in hand, green robe, holly wreath.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me!

SCROOGE
Spirit, conduct me where you will.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

They stand in city streets. People shovel snow, carry dinners. The Ghost sprinkles incense from his torch.

They enter the Cratchit home.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - NIGHT

MRS. CRATCHIT and BELINDA lay the cloth. PETER mashes potatoes. Two small Cratchits dance. MARTHA arrives. BOB CRATCHIT enters with TINY TIM on his shoulder.

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

They eat the goose and pudding. Bob proposes a toast.

BOB CRATCHIT
I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon.

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear, Christmas Day.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

SCROOGE
No, no, kind Spirit! Say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
Oh, no, kind Spirit!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head. The Ghost shows miners, a lighthouse, a ship, then Fred's party.

INT. FRED'S HOME - NIGHT

FRED laughs.

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

FRED'S NIECE
More shame for him, Fred!

They play blind-man's buff, forfeits, Yes and No. Fred thinks of Scrooge.

FRED
Uncle Scrooge!

The Ghost grows older. Two children, IGNORANCE and WANT, cling to its robe.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, and all of their degree, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom, unless the writing be erased.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes twelve. The Ghost vanishes.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME, tall, hooded, points onward.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future! I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. But as I know your purpose is to do me good, I am prepared to bear you company. Lead on!

They move among merchants discussing a dead man no one mourns. Thieves sell his belongings. A woman prays in relief at his death. Scrooge sees a body under a sheet.

SCROOGE
Spirit, this is a fearful place. Let us go!

The Ghost points to the head. Scrooge begs to see tenderness connected with a death. They enter the Cratchit home.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - NIGHT

The family sits quiet. Bob returns from the churchyard.

BOB CRATCHIT
I wish you could have gone. It would have done you good to see how green a place it is. But you'll see it often. I promised him that I would walk there on a Sunday. My little, little child!

They comfort one another. Bob praises Fred's kindness.

BOB CRATCHIT
If I can be of service to you in any way, he said, giving me his card, that's where I live. Pray come to me.

SCROOGE
Spectre, something informs me that our parting moment is at hand. Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead?

The Ghost takes him to his office, then a churchyard. It points to a grave.

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

The Ghost points downward. Scrooge reads his own name.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed? No, Spirit! Oh no, no! Spirit, hear me! I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse. Why show me this, if I am past all hope? Good Spirit, assure me that I yet may change these shadows you have shown me, by an altered life! I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

He catches the spectral hand. The Phantom shrinks into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - DAY

Scrooge clutches the bedpost.

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this!

He laughs, dresses in his best, opens the window.

SCROOGE
What's to-day?

BOY
To-day! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it. The Spirits have done it all in one night. Hallo, my fine fellow! Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one? Go and buy the big prize Turkey, and tell 'em to bring it here. Come back with him in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The boy runs. Scrooge meets the Portly Gentleman.

SCROOGE
My dear sir, how do you do? I hope you succeeded yesterday. A merry Christmas to you, sir!

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Mr. Scrooge?

SCROOGE
Yes. Allow me to ask your pardon. Not a farthing less than this.

He whispers. The gentleman is astonished.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Come and see me.

Scrooge goes to Fred's house.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - DAY

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred shakes his arm off. The party is wonderful.

INT. COUNTING HOUSE - DAY

Scrooge waits at his desk. Bob arrives late.

SCROOGE
What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. It shall not be repeated.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend, I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore I am about to raise your salary!

Bob stares.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you, for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family, and we will discuss your affairs this very afternoon, over a Christmas bowl of smoking bishop, Bob! Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

Scrooge was better than his word. He did it all, and infinitely more; and to Tiny Tim, who did not die, he was a second father. He became as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man, as the good old city knew. His own heart laughed: and that was quite enough for him.

He had no further intercourse with Spirits, but lived upon the Total Abstinence Principle, ever afterwards; and it was always said of him, that he knew how to keep Christmas well, if any man alive possessed the knowledge. May that be truly said of us, and all of us!

And so, as Tiny Tim observed, God bless Us, Every One!

THE END
